      "index": 1,
      "speaker_id": "moderator",
      "speaker_name": "Moderator",
      "text": "Today we're deciding: Agree on a quest reward system for our tabletop RPG campaign.\nFor this simulated decision, I’ll treat the following setup as the shared facts.\nOptions:\nA) Equal Gold Split After Each Quest — complexity: low; distribution speed: immediate after quest; player engagement: moderate (+ Simple and fair, everyone gets the same amount quickly; − No differentiation for effort or contribution)\nB) Performance-Based Item Rewards — complexity: high; distribution speed: delayed until session end; player engagement: high (+ Rewards individual effort and creativity with unique items; − Takes longer to decide and may cause disagreements)\nC) Quest Points Traded for Custom Perks — complexity: medium; distribution speed: accumulated over multiple quests; player engagement: variable (+ Flexible system allowing players to choose rewards that fit…; − Requires tracking and agreement on perk values)\nD) Randomized Loot Table Drops — complexity: medium; distribution speed: immediate after quest; player engagement: high excitement (+ Adds unpredictability and excitement to rewards; − Luck can overshadow effort and cause frustration)\nContext: The group has exactly 3 players; The campaign is expected to last several months with weekly sessions; The goal is to keep all players engaged and motivated without slowing down gameplay.\nHow should we balance fairness, excitement, and simplicity in our quest rewards?",
